WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.580
How do you turn a video of a human hand

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.251
into a demonstration a robot can learn from?

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.730
With just the first frame and a text prompt, the hand goes straight for the slot.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.500
Our sketch calls for a miss.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.823
The generated hand misses to the right, slides across, and inserts the key.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.200
The video shows a bottle moving across the image.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.040
How far did it actually travel?

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.670
Each tick on the sketch is five centimeters.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.584
That motion gives the robot a distance reference.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.880
The agent uses color and depth to place the bottle and cup.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.740
It selects a patch on the bottle for contact,

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:43.152
then plans a path that clears the rim, tilts the bottle, and sets it down.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.260
The rendered sketch guides the motion.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.485
The video model supplies the fingers and the scene appearance.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.660
Put the can in the bowl.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.640
Here are sixteen ways to lay out those same objects.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.170
Each generated view takes the place of its matching sketch,

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.541
so you can compare their starting positions.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.960
One hand bumps the bottle aside. Another knocks it over.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.250
Both back away before trying again.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.650
Here, the hand misses its grasp, or loses the bottle after lifting.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.272
Each sketch specifies a different retry.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.980
Watch the key tip. Text alone leads to a direct approach.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.760
When the sketch calls for a sideways correction,

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.841
the generated hand slides the key along the lock face before insertion.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.860
The generated hand can move ahead of the sketch.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.842
Dynamic time warping matches each video frame to a planned object pose.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.710
The video tells us which skin patches touch.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:44.491
The sketch tells us which part of the moving bottle those fingers should stay against.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.450
We fit one hand scale for the clip and a wrist pose for each frame.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:53.300
Then we refine the wrist and finger angles to match the image

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.243
and reduce gaps and penetration.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.390
We solve for the robot arm and finger joints together.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:03.450
Finger pads reach their contact targets with opposing surface normals,

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.832
while the optimizer penalizes penetration.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.790
Starting from its own grasp,

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:11.837
the robot follows the relative wrist motion through the rest of the action.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.920
A pinch at the tissue edge, a grasp around a handle, a lift over a tray.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.792
Each generated observation has a corresponding robot action.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.730
Sketch reaches twenty-two percent overall closed-loop success.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.842
New objects are harder for the policy than new layouts.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:33.740
The same procedure produces demonstrations for a parallel gripper and two dexterous hands.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:36.835
Each robot solves the grasp using its own geometry.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.360
On the real robot, a policy trained on Sketch data

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.805
grasps the bottle and pours into the bowl.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.090
With more layouts and six hundred forty-eight Sketch demonstrations,

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.550
the policy succeeds in forty-one of a hundred trials across five tasks.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.821
The drawer and laptop policies were trained separately.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.437
Thank you for watching.
